{
  "An abacus is a counting tool made of rods and beads. Each bead position stands for a number.": "sent/e58dij.mp3",
  "A rod is a vertical wire that holds the beads. Each rod stands for one place value, like units, tens, or hundreds.": "sent/yaet6a.mp3",
  "A bead is a small piece that slides on a rod. Its position tells us what number it shows.": "sent/y49csb.mp3",
  "The beam is the horizontal bar in the middle. Beads touching the beam are counted. Beads away from the beam are not counted.": "sent/c243f2.mp3",
  "The heaven bead sits above the beam. When it touches the beam, it is worth five.": "sent/mci7q.mp3",
  "The earth beads sit below the beam. Each one touching the beam is worth one. There are four on every rod.": "sent/xo930h.mp3",
  "Place value means each rod has its own worth. The rod on the right is units, the next is tens, then hundreds, and so on.": "sent/7twxpi.mp3",
  "A carry means we pass one to the next rod on the left, because the current rod is full.": "sent/yif86v.mp3",
  "A borrow means we take one from the next rod on the left, because the current rod does not have enough.": "sent/1ugc77f.mp3",
  "A complement is the number that completes a target. The five complement of 3 is 2, because 3 plus 2 makes 5. The ten complement of 3 is 7, because 3 plus 7 makes 10.": "sent/xncf78.mp3",
  "The five complement method helps when there are not enough earth beads. We use the heaven bead, worth five, and fix the difference.": "sent/j2004o.mp3",
  "The ten complement method helps when a rod goes past nine or runs short. We move one on the next rod, worth ten here, and fix the difference.": "sent/1v93415.mp3",
  "Clearing the abacus means moving every bead away from the beam, so every rod shows zero.": "sent/a6d8nz.mp3",
  "Welcome to the abacus. Let's learn together, one step at a time.": "sent/12lev5a.mp3",
  "The abacus is clear. All rods show zero.": "sent/k1ziwi.mp3",
